**(a) Theorem NR-q — MAJOR proof bookkeeping gap; padding itself passes.**

The padded split works:

- \(2X^2\le 2\cdot4^kN<9^{k+1}N=N_*\).
- For odd \(N\), \(3\nmid N\), the square-class index is \(2^{\omega(N)+1}\).
- Eventually \(Y>3\), so the target primes are coprime to \(N_*\).
- S″ gives the claimed exponent, with only \(\exp(-O(k))\) additional loss.
- The halves remain \(N^{1/2+o(1)}\), and CRT preserves conditional hidden-root uniformity modulo \(N\).

Explicitly condition sampling on units modulo \(N_*\), with the usual gcd exits/rejections; uniform unrestricted \(r\) does not directly give S″’s sampling law.

The **total-work requirement** now correctly includes failed branches and backtracking.

However, §8 step 1 defines \(p_q\) using **factor exit or tree**, while \(G_{\rm pool}\) contains **trees only**. Consequently,
\[
\Pr(q\notin G_{\rm pool})\le(1-p_q)^{J_f}
\]
does not follow. Apply the pooling argument to targets for which some pool call returns **either a tree or a factor exit**, and propagate that disjunction through split acceptance. This repairs the argument without strengthening T\(_q\).

The doubling arithmetic is correct:
\[
p_0=0.72252654\ldots,\qquad 2(1-p_0)=0.55494692\ldots<1.
\]
But \(T^*\) must mean expected time **until a verified factor**, including character retries and fresh-row retries after trivial gcds. Completing one LV6 batch is insufficient: it still has the fruitfulness failure probability. State that inner retry loop explicitly.

**(b) Prop R3 — tree proof passes; MINOR DAG proof omission.**

For trees, the corrected recurrence, constant \(2(1+2\kappa)\), \(\kappa\ge2\), and degree-three argument are sound. The claim that \(\kappa=2\) covers \(d+1\) points is correctly restricted to \(d\ge5\).

The DAG remark does not justify the same unrolling: shared descendants occur along multiple paths. In particular, reusing the same ideal does not make its target logarithm the sum of its incoming occurrences.

The qualitative conclusion is repairable. Under the displayed edge recurrence, an acyclic component with \(n\) distinct nodes satisfies
\[
X_v\le \frac d{d-2}\left(1+\frac2{d-2}\right)^{n-1}\Lambda U
\le 2e^{2\kappa}\Lambda U\quad(d\ge4).
\]
Thus the \(O_\kappa(\Lambda U)\) bound and \(2/5\) floor survive; supply this argument or restrict the proof to trees.

**(c) Chain envelope — correct; MINOR wording error.**

The piecewise fixed-depth formula and its lower envelope are correct. So are the plateau endpoints
\[
\left[r+1-\frac1{r+1},\ r+2-\frac1{r+1}\right]
\]
and \(\alpha^*(13/5)=7/19\).

Exact-rational checks passed at 115 fixed-depth optima around plateau transitions, plus the \(13/5\) example.

“Level \(r\) is optimal **exactly** on the plateau” is false: it is also optimal along its preceding descending segment. Replace this with “the plateau at value \((r+2)/(3r+5)\) is exactly…”.

**(d) Exhaustive-reduction claim — FIXED; no adverse finding.**

Both the Verdict and §9 now explicitly present a catalogue of inspected failures and sufficient hypotheses. Tied-field randomness, directed recombination and structured image certificates remain open alternatives.

**(e) Other new or residual errors.**

- **MAJOR — newly weakened Bach hypothesis.** Verdict item 4 and §4 replace ERH by RH for \(\zeta_K\) alone. The cited class-group generation result requires RH for the relevant class-character Hecke \(L\)-functions; the weaker assertion is unsupported. See the explicit hypothesis in [Grenié–Molteni, Theorem 2.4](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1607.02430).
- **MINOR — endpoint scope mismatch.** §8 requires \(d\asymp D^\delta\), \(\delta<\alpha\), but then claims to contain nested’s \(\alpha=1/3,\ d\asymp D^{1/3}\) theorem. That boundary case needs separate admission; the parenthetical character-cost calculation already anticipates it.
- **MINOR — excluded ramification.** The union of the source polynomial discriminants bounds ramification of their common normal closure, not an arbitrary larger Galois \(L\). Restrict \(L\) accordingly or charge its additional ramification.

**Overall verdict:** **NR-q: CORRECT WITH FIXES, not yet fully confirmed as written.** The central padded-split repair is valid; the factor-exit success event and successful-factor restart loop need explicit correction. No FATAL defect was found. The note’s revised scoped conclusion survives, with the qualifications above; it establishes no unconditional improvement below \(L[2/5]\).